      {
        "id": "s5_eggs",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Eggs and larvae in soil",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Eggs",
        "lines": [
          "After copulation in the small intestine the",
          "female releases about 200,000 eggs every day.",
          "Each egg is surrounded by a rippled protein",
          "coat, so it is called a mammillated egg.",
          "The eggs are passed out along with the faecal",
          "matter.",
          "In moist soil the development takes place inside",
          "the egg and the first stage rhabditiform larva",
          "is formed.",
          "It undergoes the first moulting and the second",
          "stage rhabditiform larva is formed. This second",
          "stage larva is the infective stage to man.",
          "These eggs reach a new host through contaminated",
          "food and water."
        ],
        "why": "The parasite has no second host, so the soil does the work instead: the egg has to develop outside the body before it can infect anyone, and that is why the second-stage larva is the infective form.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the egg is infective as soon as it is passed out. It must develop in moist soil first.",
          "Calling the first stage larva infective. The SECOND stage rhabditiform larva is infective.",
          "Writing that the larva hatches in the soil. It stays inside the egg until it is swallowed."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Two larval stages in the soil, and the second one is the one that infects.",
        "margin_note": "Six short lines from egg to infective stage. Give the number 200,000."
      },
